(project pricklypear-structural-editing
 (doc "Pricklypear book chapter: structural editing — paredit-shaped tree surgery as one shared protocol over outlines and code ASTs")
 (status partial)
 (* agent note (|2026-08-02 status reconcile: structed-algebra+op-protocol+keymap+non-goals implemented; only ast-structure-view remains planned — keeps project/section partial.|) *)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section structural-editing
  (doc "Structural editing: named tree ops over one shared protocol — outlines and code ASTs are edited as trees, never as paren text")
  (status partial)
  (details
   (doc (|
        =========================================================
        PRODUCT INTENT
        =========================================================
        Programs are trees. Outlines are trees. Editing either one
        should be TREE SURGERY with named operations — slurp, barf,
        wrap, splice, raise — never character editing over a
        serialized rendering. This chapter defines ONE interaction
        protocol for structural editing, shared by the notes
        outline editor (notes.hypermedia-ui) and the function
        editor's structure view (image.function-editor), and later
        by any tree-shaped surface.

        The paredit vocabulary (slurp / barf / wrap / splice /
        raise / split / join / transpose) is the headline, but the
        chapter is really three rules:

          RULE 1 — You type inside atoms; you never type structure.
            contenteditable is for LEAVES (titles, bodies, atom
            text). Structure changes only via named commands.
            Unbalanced structure is unrepresentable.

          RULE 2 — The server is authoritative for structure.
            The browser sends (op, target, args); the image applies
            a PURE function to the tree and returns a re-render
            plus the new selection. The client MAY apply that same
            pure op locally for immediate feedback via the browser
            evaluator (client-eval) — the result is COMPUTED by the
            same source the server runs, not predicted from a
            divergent client codebase — and reconciles to server
            truth by morph on response. What is forbidden is
            structural prediction from a SECOND, hand-maintained
            client implementation of the op algebra (the lie).

          RULE 3 — Selection is part of the op algebra.
            Every op returns (tree', selection'): where the cursor
            lands after delete/slurp/wrap is computed by the same
            pure function that computed the tree, and travels back
            with the response.

        STORAGE IS UNCHANGED. Outlines stay JSON strings in
        data_rows; function bodies stay JSONB in function_defs.
        The unification is the value model (nested lists/atoms)
        and the op protocol — NOT a storage codec migration.
        Whole-document write remains the conflict domain.

        =========================================================
        SUBSECTIONS
        =========================================================
        structed-algebra     — pure higher-order op library
        op-protocol          — the POST contract + selection OOB
        keymap               — chords now, modal target
        ast-structure-view   — /edit Text|Structure toggle
        client-eval          — browser evaluator: same code, optimistic execution
        non-goals            — what this chapter refuses to build
   |)))
  (subsection structed-algebra
   (doc "Pure higher-order tree-surgery library (structed) usable from REPL, routes, and agents")
   (status implemented)
   (implements libs/structed/lib.pp)
   (details
    (doc (|
         PACKAGE: structed/ (Nopales — pure, no DB, no I/O).
         Load: (load-library "structed").

         HIGHER-ORDER OVER CHILD ACCESS. One op implementation
         serves any tree whose domain supplies child accessors:

           (structed/slurp-forward get-kids set-kids tree target)

         The outline backend passes (outline/kids, outline/set-kids);
         the AST backend passes expr-children accessors. Do NOT
         duplicate op logic per domain, and do NOT force one node
         shape across domains.

         OP SET (v1):
           slurp-forward    target absorbs its next sibling as new last child
           slurp-backward   target absorbs its prev sibling as new first child
           barf-forward     target's last child becomes its next sibling
           barf-backward    target's first child becomes its prev sibling
           wrap             target becomes sole child of a fresh node
                            (fresh-node shape is domain-supplied; the
                            expr domain takes an optional head-atom arg:
                            (wrap t "f") on x -> (f x), so wrapping
                            never needs an empty head — see
                            ast-structure-view)
           splice           target removed; its children take its place, in order
           raise            target replaces its parent
           split            target divides at a position into two adjacent siblings
           join             target merges with its next sibling
           transpose        target swaps with an adjacent sibling
         The existing outline ops promote / demote / move-up /
         move-down are the outline-domain special cases of this
         vocabulary and stay where they are.

         TOTALITY: every op is total. Unknown target, missing
         sibling, root-guard violation, wrong shape -> identity
         tree and UNCHANGED selection. A structure key must never
         produce an error page.

         RETURN SHAPE: (list tree' selection'). Selection rules:
           slurp / barf / wrap / transpose : selection stays on target
           splice                          : first lifted child, else parent
           raise                           : stays on target (now at parent's place)
           delete (domain op)              : next sibling, else prev, else parent
           add-child / add-sibling         : the new node

         PROPERTIES TO TEST (Nopales-level via load-string + eval; no
         services, no browser):
           slurp-forward then barf-forward  = identity on the tree
           wrap then splice                 = identity on the tree
           any op on empty / atom / unknown target = identity
    |)))
   (verify
    (smoke "(load-library \"structed\") exposes structed/*")
    (smoke "slurp+barf and wrap+splice round-trip to identity on a sample tree")
    (smoke "ops against root/unknown/empty targets return input unchanged")))
  (subsection op-protocol
   (doc "One POST contract: (op, target, args) -> re-render + out-of-band selection")
   (status implemented)
   (implements libs/notes/lib.pp libs/outline/lib.pp)
   (details
    (doc (|
         WIRE: structure edits and leaf edits are the same protocol.

           POST {op, target, field args...}
           -> 200 HTML: re-rendered tree fragment (outerHTML swap)
              + out-of-band selection state (hidden element swapped
              via hx-swap-oob carrying the new selection address).

         The client shell restores highlight + caret after each
         swap. This extends the notes/outline/apply-op pattern:
         dispatch by op name to a pure fn; unknown op -> (tree,
         same selection), still a 200 re-render.

         SURGICAL WIRE, WHOLE-DOC STORAGE. Payloads name a node
         and small args — never the whole document. The server
         decodes the whole tree, applies the pure op, encodes,
         writes the whole document (data_rows field or
         function_defs body). The DOCUMENT is the conflict domain;
         no per-node storage in v1.

         ABSTRACT TARGET IDENTITY. Outline surfaces address nodes
         by stable id (already true). The AST surface addresses
         nodes by PATH (data-path="0.2.1") until
         branching.ast-nodes provides stable node ids. Op
         responses always return the selection's CURRENT address,
         so path invalidation after reorder is handled by
         construction. Upgrading identity (paths -> ids) must not
         change the protocol or the client.

         RESPONSIVENESS VIA SAME-CODE EXECUTION. The client runs the
         same pure op locally through the browser evaluator
         (client-eval) for immediate feedback, then the server
         confirms authoritatively and the response is MORPHED in —
         never merged field-by-field with the prediction, never
         patching client state. Because the local result is computed
         by identical source, the divergence window is server-only
         effects (validation, concurrency), not code drift; an op-seq
         guards staleness so an out-of-order response cannot clobber
         a newer local state. The htmx-org hx-optimistic extension
         (template placeholder, swapped wholesale on response) remains
         available for non-structural previews but is not the
         structural mechanism. The client shell may ignore structure
         keys while one op POST is in flight (single-flight), and
         restores selection + focus from the OOB payload on completion.

         FLUSH RULE. Any structure POST first flushes the focused
         leaf's pending edit (set-title / set-body / set-atom) —
         either folded into the same request or as a forced
         blur-commit — so a tree re-render can never destroy
         in-progress leaf text. This replaces any narrower
         "commit on blur" rule.

         UNDO. Whole-tree snapshots per op are the undo model —
         trees are persistent values, snapshots are cheap. Ring
         placement (client or server session) is v1 implementation
         discretion.
    |)))
   (verify
    (smoke "POST outline op returns tree partial + OOB selection element")
    (smoke "delete selects next sibling; at end of siblings selects parent")
    (smoke "unknown op name re-renders unchanged tree with 200")))
  (subsection keymap
   (doc "Chord-first keyboard map; modal structure/text interaction is the documented target")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-02: v1 Alt chords shipped in notes outline script (libs/notes/lib.pp): Alt+Up/Down move, Alt+Shift+Left/Right promote/demote, Alt+Left/Right barf/slurp, Alt+Enter sibling; preventDefault. Modal map still documented target not v1.|) *)
   (implements libs/notes/lib.pp)
   (details
    (doc (|
         V1 — MODIFIER CHORDS ONLY. Plain keys always edit text
         when a caret sits inside a contenteditable leaf; structure
         never fires on an unmodified key. Required chord set
         (outline surface; AST surface mirrors):

           Alt+ArrowUp / Alt+ArrowDown   transpose with prev / next sibling
           Alt+Shift+ArrowLeft / Right   promote / demote
           Alt+ArrowRight                slurp-forward
           Alt+ArrowLeft                 barf-forward
           Alt+Enter                     add sibling

         The full op set stays reachable via toolbar/menu.
         No structure key fires during IME composition.

         CHORD CONFLICT (known — verify early). Alt+ArrowLeft /
         Alt+ArrowRight is browser history back/forward on Linux
         and Windows. The map relies on preventDefault winning
         when the editor has focus: VERIFY THIS FIRST in the
         keymap work, in the target browsers, before building
         more on it. If preventDefault does not hold, slurp/barf
         move to Alt+Shift+ArrowLeft/Right and promote/demote
         relocate (e.g. Alt+, / Alt+.) — update the table then.
         Do not add per-OS keymaps in v1.

         TARGET — MODAL (not v1 acceptance): a structure mode (a
         whole node is selected; plain keys move selection and
         operate; Enter enters the title as text) and a text mode
         (caret in leaf; Esc returns to structure mode). Server and
         protocol are mode-agnostic — modal is a pure client
         evolution. Client code MUST keep a distinct keymap layer
         (a data-driven chord table, not scattered hardcoded
         checks) so the modal map can replace the chord map without
         touching op dispatch.

         ONE CLIENT SHELL. Selection state, postOp, blur-commit,
         restore-after-swap, and the keymap layer are ONE script
         pattern shared by the outline editor and the AST
         structure view — generalized from the notes outline
         script, not forked per surface.
    |)))
   (verify
    (smoke "Alt+ArrowRight on a selected node slurps its next sibling")
    (smoke "Alt+ArrowLeft/Right never trigger browser history navigation while the editor is focused")
    (smoke "plain keys with caret inside a leaf edit text only")
    (smoke "structure op in flight: second chord ignored; selection restored after swap")))
  (subsection ast-structure-view
   (doc "Function editor gains a Text | Structure toggle over the same function_defs row")
   (status planned)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-02 confirm still planned: no Text|Structure toggle on /edit/<name>; function editor remains textarea. structed algebra + outline op-protocol ready as substrate.|) *)
   (implements image/lib/http_pages.ml image/lib/session.ml)
   (details
    (doc (|
         /edit/<name> gains a view toggle: Text (existing textarea,
         parse whole body on save) | Structure (AST rendered as
         addressable nested HTML — the outline/html-node ADDRESSING
         pattern (data-path on every node) applied to exprs, NOT
         the outline's row layout). Lists are structural
         containers; atoms are contenteditable leaves.

         LAYOUT IS A PRETTY-PRINT, NOT AN OUTLINE. The structure
         view must NOT render one node per line — that would make
         (+ 1 2) taller than it is wide and code unreadable. The
         renderer is a pretty-printer over the AST:
           - atoms, and lists that fit within a width budget,
             render INLINE on one line: (+ 1 2), (dict-get u "name")
           - a list breaks vertical only when over budget, or by
             form-aware convention (define / lambda / let / cond
             bodies one form per line; operator applications stay
             horizontal while they fit)
           - vertical children indent by depth; all whitespace is
             derived, never stored — the AST carries no layout bytes
         Selection and data-path addressing are layout-independent:
         a node keeps the same path whether its children flow
         horizontally or vertically, and structural ops behave
         identically either way. Layout direction is a per-domain
         RENDER decision — outlines render one node per row because
         that IS the outline reading experience; code renders as a
         pretty-print. The shared op protocol does not care.

         BUILD ORDER (layout):
           1. VERTICAL RENDERER FIRST: every list breaks vertical,
              one node per line — the degenerate pretty-printer
              (width budget zero). Easiest correct render, and it
              exercises the hard parts (data-path addressing,
              selection restore, ops, atom edits, re-render) before
              any layout engineering. NOT a throwaway: because
              addressing and ops are layout-independent, stage 2
              changes only the renderer.
           2. INLINE PACKING: introduce the width budget and
              form-aware conventions; short forms collapse onto one
              line. Pure renderer change — protocol, ops, paths,
              and the client shell are untouched.

         BACKEND: the same structed algebra with expr accessors.
         The AST is an expr tree (App / Lambda / Let / If / Quote
         ...), NOT a tree of VLists: child access is defined PER
         CONSTRUCTOR as the subexpressions in reader/surface
         order (App -> head then args; Lambda -> params then
         body; If -> test, then-branch, else-branch). Quoted
         literals are OPAQUE LEAVES in v1 — no descending into
         quoted data. The exact per-constructor child table is
         implementation discretion within that constraint.
         Domain differences: wrap takes an optional head-atom —
         (wrap t "f") on x -> (f x); with no arg it wraps in a
         fresh one-element list. split/join operate on list
         element runs; there is no promote/demote — raise/splice
         cover that space.

         ATOM EDITS ARE TRANSACTIONAL AND TYPE-AWARE. The
         renderer tags each leaf with its atom type. Blur sends
         set-atom {path, text}; the server validates PER TYPE:
           - string leaves: text is accepted RAW — spaces and
             parens are data, not syntax
           - symbol / number / bool leaves: text must read-string
             as exactly one atom of a compatible shape, else the
             old value is kept and the tree re-renders unchanged
           - converting a leaf's TYPE (symbol -> string, number
             -> symbol) is an explicit op, never a side effect
             of editing text
         Because invalid text never enters the AST, HOLES are not
         needed in v1; a hole node type is a possible later
         extension, deliberately out of scope here.

         SAVE IS UNCHANGED: whole function body, JSONB, via the
         existing value<->JSON codec and the function_defs row.
         The structure view is a second editor over the same
         storage — not a new persistence path.

         AI AUTHORING: op + path + args with whole-body write is
         the surgical patch surface ai-authoring asks for, ahead
         of branching.ast-nodes. When ast-nodes lands, paths
         upgrade to stable ids behind the same protocol (see
         op-protocol abstract identity).
    |)))
   (verify
    (smoke "GET /edit/<fn> structure view renders nested tree with data-path leaves")
    (smoke "layout stage 2: a short form like (+ 1 2) renders on ONE line, not one node per line")
    (smoke "layout stage 2: define/lambda body breaks vertical by convention; selection paths stable across both layouts")
    (smoke "set-atom with unparseable text keeps old value and re-renders")
    (smoke "set-atom on a string leaf accepts raw text containing spaces and parens")
    (smoke "slurp via POST re-renders; saved body reflects the change on reload")))
  (subsection client-eval
   (doc "Browser build of the Nopales evaluator runs the same pure op algebra the server runs, so an optimistic edit is a real local computation — not a predicted guess — reconciled to server truth by morph")
   (status implemented)
   (implements lisp/lib pure/prims_pure.ml client/bin/main.ml client/conformance/main.ml client/lib/pp_client_table.ml libs/structed/lib.pp libs/outline/lib.pp libs/html/lib.pp)
   (details
    (doc (|
         SAME EVALUATOR, SECOND TARGET. The pure interpreter core
         (lisp/lib: reader + ast + eval + env, no I/O) is compiled
         to JavaScript via js_of_ocaml. OCaml 5 effects require the
         CPS backend: every JS build passes --effects=cps, or it
         fatally aborts. The same .pp package source the server
         serves is evaluated verbatim in the browser — structed,
         outline, html ride along as data, never reimplemented.

         SAME SOURCE AT RUNTIME, not at build. The browser fetches
         the current branch's package source on page load (the
         image is the API) and evaluates it, so a user who
         redefines structed/barf-forward on their personal branch
         has their browser run their version. Build-embedded
         source is a boot fallback only — it would drift the
         moment a promote lands without a redeploy, breaking the
         branching chapter's live-source invariant.

         PURE PRIM TABLE ONLY. The browser prim table registers
         the pure prims and EXCLUDES every DB / IO / net prim.
         The pure/DB split already exists in code (prims_pure.ml
         vs prims_db.ml); this subsection makes it structural —
         prims_pure is factored into a library both image/ and
         client/ depend on, so the two runtimes cannot drift on
         what "pure" means. Each registered prim name is bound as
         VPrim in the browser env, mirroring session.ml's
         auto-bind loop, so (list ...), structed/*, outline/*
         resolve. The capability boundary IS the prim table: a
         structed op cannot touch Postgres because those prims are
         simply unbound.

         OPTIMISTIC EXECUTION MODEL. On a structure op:
           1. client applies the same pure op fn to its local tree
           2. renders via the same render lib (libs/html / outline)
           3. morphs the result into the DOM with a pp-in-transit
              class on the affected subtree (grey = unconfirmed,
              the chat-app sending/sent indicator — never a claim
              of truth)
           4. fires the real POST carrying an op-seq
           5. server: stale seq -> 409, client morphs server truth
              back; else persist + return server-rendered fragment
           6. client morphs the fragment in; class clears. If the
              two renders agree (same code, deterministic) the
              morph is a no-op except grey -> normal.
           error -> toast + morph to server truth.

         OP-SEQ. A monotonic sequence per editing session travels
         with each op; the server rejects out-of-order ops so a
         late response cannot clobber a newer local state.
         Single-user edits serialize naturally. Multi-user
         concurrency is unchanged from op-protocol: the document
         is the conflict domain, last-write-wins on whole-doc
         save.

         WHY THIS IS NOT THE LIE. The objection to optimistic
         updates (optimistic updates are a rendering trick
         pretending to be application truth) lands when the
         optimistic result is a GUESS from a second codebase.
         Here the local result is produced by the SAME evaluator
         running the SAME source; the only divergence sources
         left are server-only effects (validation, uniqueness,
         concurrent writers) — exactly what the op-seq + morph +
         toast path exists to surface. There is no second
         implementation to drift.

         CONFORMANCE IS THE GATE. The lisp conformance suite
         (lisp/test/conformance_test.ml) runs against the JS
         target, so client/server agreement is a tested invariant,
         not a hope. The client-eval subsection is not accepted
         until that gate is green.

         NOT A REPLACEMENT FOR THE PROTOCOL. The op-protocol POST
         contract, OOB selection, flush rule, and single-flight
         shell are unchanged. client-eval only changes what the
         client does BETWEEN keypress and response: compute
         locally with the same code, instead of showing a static
         placeholder.
    |)))
   (verify
    (build "dune build client/ yields interp.js; js_of_ocaml invoked with --effects=cps")
    (test "browser evaluator passes lisp/test/conformance_test.ml against the JS target")
    (smoke "page load fetches current-branch structed/outline/html source and evaluates it without error")
    (smoke "op click: local apply renders grey; server confirm morphs and clears grey; matching renders make the morph a no-op")
    (smoke "stale op-seq: server 409, client morphs server truth back")
    (smoke "op error: toast shown and DOM morphed to server truth")))
  (subsection non-goals
   (doc "What this chapter deliberately does not build")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-02: documentation-only; implemented as spec-complete.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         - NO paredit-over-text: no paren surgery inside a textarea
           or CodeMirror buffer. The tree is the editor; text
           buffers remain for the Text view and /code browsing.
         - NO one-node-per-line as the FINAL code layout: vertical
           rendering is build stage 1 (degenerate pretty-printer),
           not the accepted end state — the end state is inline
           flow with width/convention breaks (ast-structure-view).
         - NO structural prediction from a DIVERGENT client codebase
           (op-protocol). Same-evaluator local execution (client-eval)
           is sanctioned because the result is computed by identical
           source, not guessed; what remains forbidden is a second,
           hand-maintained client implementation of the op algebra.
           Display-placeholder optimism via the hx-optimistic extension
           stays allowed for non-structural previews the response
           replaces wholesale.
         - NO storage codec migration: outlines stay JSON strings
           in data_rows; code ASTs stay JSONB in function_defs. A
           canonical sexp writer prim (write-string) for
           agent-facing output may be added independently under
           lisp-interpreter.primitives; this chapter does not
           depend on it.
         - NO per-node storage and NO multi-user concurrent node
           editing: the document is the conflict domain.
         - Modal keymap is the documented target, not v1
           acceptance (keymap).
         - Hole AST nodes (ast-structure-view).
         - SINGLE-NODE SELECTION ONLY in v1: no ranges, no
           multi-select, no region ops.
         - KILL / YANK (subtree cut-copy-paste, ideally as sexp
           text on the system clipboard) is deferred, not ruled
           out — it is how larger code moves will work later.
         - STALE WRITES: whole-document save makes two tabs on
           one document last-write-wins in v1. This inherits
           image.function-editor's deferred optimistic-
           concurrency / stale-body-reject item; resolve both
           together.
    |))))
 )
)
